          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nClaude Lévi-Strauss was born on November 28, 1908, in Brussels, Belgium. He studied law and philosophy at the University of Paris. His early academic work did not hint at his future as an anthropologist, a field in which he would later become a leading figure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Time in Brazil and Early Fieldwork</strong><br>\nIn the 1930s, Lévi-Strauss taught sociology at the University of São Paulo in Brazil and conducted research on the indigenous tribes of the Amazon Basin. This experience was pivotal, shaping his future theoretical work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Structural Anthropology</strong><br>\nLévi-Strauss's experiences in Brazil led to the development of structural anthropology. He posited that human cultures, including their myths and social organization, are structured like languages, with universal patterns underpinning all cultural phenomena.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
